ales]])\n+                   LogicalTableScan(table=[[catalog_sales]])\n? ++\n\n+                 LogicalFilter(condition=[=($15(cr_reason_sk), 5)])\n-                 LogicalTableScan(table=[[catalog_returns]])\n+                   LogicalTableScan(table=[[catalog_returns]])\n? ++\n\n-               LogicalTableScan(table=[[warehouse]])\n? --\n\n+             LogicalTableScan(table=[[warehouse]])\n+           LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=(CAST($12(i_category)):CHAR(5), 'Books'), SEARCH($20(i_manager_id), Sarg[[61..100]]))])\n              LogicalTableScan(table=[[item]])\n+         LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(>=($2(d_date), -(2001-05-21, 2592000000:INTERVAL DAY)), <=($2(d_date), +(2001-05-21, 2592000000:INTERVAL DAY)))])\n            LogicalTableScan(table=[[date_dim]])\n  \n```"}], 'response': "Here's the explanation of how the query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query:\n\nStep 1: The logical plan changes after rewriting indicate that the query has been rewritten by applying the query rewrite rule. Specifically, the rule has transformed the original query into a new query with different join conditions and filter predicates.\n\nStep 2: Parsing the logical plan changes reveals that the query rewrite rule has applied Case 2 of the rule to the original query. This case applies when a filter condition applies solely to columns from one side of an OUTER JOIN, in this case, the `catalog_returns` table.\n\nStep 3: The parsed SQL query changes show that the original query had a LEFT OUTER JOIN between `catalog_sales` and `catalog_returns`, with a WHERE clause filtering on columns from both tables. The rewritten query has transformed this join into an INNER JOIN by pushing the filter predicate into the ON clause of the join.\n\nStep 4: The rewritten query now has an INNER JOIN between `catalog_sales` and `catalog_returns`, with the filter predicate applied to the `catalog_returns` table. This transformation is justified because the original WHERE clause only referenced columns from the `catalog_returns` table, making it safe to push the filter into the ON clause of the join without altering the semantics of the query.\n\nIn summary, the query rewrite rule has applied Case 2 to transform a LEFT OUTER JOIN with a filter predicate that only references columns from one side of the join into an INNER JOIN with the same filter predicate. This transformation is valid because it does not alter the expected result set and improves the efficiency of the query by reducing the size of the datasets being joined.", 'time': 5.945467472076416}
